The bug is an off-by-one error in the loop condition. The loop runs from i=0 to i<=len, but when i==len, it always processes the last character (even if it's not a comma). If the input ends with a comma, this creates an extra empty token, causing count to exceed MAX_TOKENS and writing beyond the tokens array. The fix is to change the loop condition to i < len, and then handle the final token after the loop if the last character is not a comma. Here is the corrected function:

```c
void parse_tokens(char* input, int len) {
    int start = 0;
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) {
        if (input[i] == ',') {
            int token_len = i - start;
            tokens[count] = (char*)malloc(token_len + 1);
            memcpy(tokens[count], input + start, token_len);
            tokens[count][token_len] = '\0';
            count++;
            start = i + 1;
        }
    }
    // Handle the last token if the input does not end with a comma
    if (start < len) {
        int token_len = len - start;
        tokens[count] = (char*)malloc(token_len + 1);
        memcpy(tokens[count], input + start, token_len);
        tokens[count][token_len] = '\0';
        count++;
    }
    tokens[count] = NULL;
}
```
This ensures that we only process delimiters within bounds, and we handle the final token separately. The original code would overflow when len=64 and the last character is a comma, because it would attempt to write a token for the empty substring after the comma, incrementing count to 65 (which is out of bounds for the tokens array). The fix prevents that.

